course: dictionary
lesson: 13305
title: Greater: bigger or more
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Greater means bigger, or more. It is the word we use to compare two things. «Nine is greater than five» tells you that 9 is the bigger number.
explain: Greater can also mean more important or better. «The greater part of the class voted for the trip» means most of the class wanted to go. Greater is the middle step of great, greater, greatest.
explain: You will meet greater in maths and in everyday talk. «There is a greater chance of rain today» means rain is more likely. Ask yourself: which is bigger or more, and does that fit the sentence?
fact: Greater means bigger or more.
fact: «Greater than» compares two things: 9 is greater than 5.
fact: The sign > means greater than, and its wide end faces the bigger number.
fact: «Greater London» means the city with all the towns around it.
fact: «Far greater» means much bigger, and «the greater part of» means most of something.
exercise: Which sign means greater than? | < | = | *> | +
exercise: «There is a greater chance of rain today.» What does greater mean here? | Smaller | *More likely | Colder | Shorter
exercise: Which number is greater: 7 or 12? | 7 | 5 | *12 | 0
test: «Her goal was far greater than anyone expected.» What does far greater mean? | Much smaller | *Much bigger | Kicked away | Just the same
test: Which sign shows that 10 is greater than 4? | 10 < 4 | 4 > 10 | 10 = 4 | *10 > 4
exam: «Greater London» means: | *The city with all the towns around it | A bigger school hall | A city with no towns near it | A kind of train
